It was three streets from the house, and he had been there twice before, both times as a curious boy reading up on something he never expected to need. Now he needed it. The building was older than the certification office and did not care how it looked. Its stone had never been refaced, and its front steps had worn into a shallow dip in the middle from centuries of feet. Inside, it smelled of paper and dust and ink that had dried long ago and had no plans to fade.

The young woman at the front desk looked barely older than Alis's brother, and when Cael gave her his registration number, her pen stopped moving. It was only for a moment before she caught it and smoothed it away. But he saw it, and he saw something else cross her face just before she smoothed that away too, something that looked very much like sympathy.

She did not ask why he had come. She took a key from a hook and led him down a passage to a door at the back and unlocked it.

"Historical classification archive. The full summary's on the second shelf from the window, bound in green." She hesitated. "The reading table's by the window. The light's better there."

"Thank you."

She went back to her desk and left the door open behind her. So much for discretion, he thought; news ran through the system faster than he could walk. She had known his number without being told, when two mornings ago, before the circle, his number had meant nothing to anyone.

The archive was one long room, shelved from floor to ceiling, with a scarred reading table and a single chair under a window that faced north, as archive windows always seemed to, as though direct sunlight might hurt something. Dust turned slowly in the pale light, and he watched it for a moment before he made himself start. The full summary was where she had said, thick and green and heavy, and the spine gave a small dry crack as he opened it, a sound that seemed much too loud in the quiet room.

He worked back from the index and found the section, not where he expected it but near the very end, under a subheading he had never seen in the community hall's copy:

*Deprecated and Non-Standard Designations, Pre-Compact Era Holdovers.*

He read the subheading once. Then he turned the page.

The entry here was not four lines but several paragraphs, in the same tight, careful print.

*[SHATTERED]: Designation assigned where Arbiter evaluation finds practitioner architecture that does not resolve to a single recognised Path. The coupling between practitioner and Path framework presents as broken or incomplete. Ability manifestation in such cases has historically been irregular and difficult to predict, and practitioner risk has been assessed as elevated.*

*Of the four (4) recorded instances, disposition is as follows: three (3) practitioners are recorded as deceased within weeks of classification, cause of death listed as "practice-related incident" in Compact records. One (1) practitioner's disposition is recorded as unknown, last registry update dated 32 years prior to the present record.*

He read the last paragraph again, and then a third time, because it was doing something he needed time to take in.

He had known the number four as you know a fact you read once and set on a shelf, and until this moment he had never turned it into people. Now he did. Four people with names, who had each stepped out of a circle somewhere and heard what he had heard, and three of them had been dead within weeks. One was not accounted for.

This was not a rule or a caution. It was an account of what had really become of every other person who had ever stood where he had stood, feet in a worn circle, counting.

*Practice-related incident.*

He looked at the phrase again, because it was doing a great deal of work. It could mean that they had died trying to use something they did not understand, or it could mean that someone had made sure of it, and he did not have the information to know which. What he suspected, without any evidence yet, was that the person who wrote it down had cared which one was believed, and that this was the only part of the sentence he could trust.

He took out his notebook and copied the entry word for word, the subheading too, in handwriting far tidier than the job needed, and he knew what that gave away. Then he sat with the green volume closed under his hands and did not read anything. He thought about the clerk at the front, young and briefly sorry before she caught herself.

He thought about how many people had sat in this chair over however many years the archive had stood, reading something that applied to someone they loved, while the room kept no trace of a single one of them, having only its shelves and its dust and the patience of paper, which outlasts everyone who ever needs it.

He put the book back exactly as he had found it, its spine level with its neighbours. At the front desk the clerk looked up as he passed and opened her mouth as if to say something, and then closed it and nodded, and he nodded back.

The street outside looked exactly as it had two hours before, which was almost the worst part. The vendors were still calling, and a cart creaked past with a load of new-cut lumber that smelled of resin, and once again the world had not noticed that his had shifted under him. He did not decide on the walk home how he felt about any of it.

He decided something else instead: he was not going to be the fifth entry in that book, and it was so easy to decide that it worried him, because deciding things on a walk home was easy and staying alive long enough to make them true was going to be the hard part.

The stew came at dark and was better than it looked, and the bread was worse than she had said. The thin woman shared her tea with him without asking whether he wanted any. He thanked her, and she said, "Long road, is it?" and he said, "Not as long as yesterday," and she seemed to think that was a good answer. He lay on the cot by the window afterward and listened to the room: the drover snoring in a long, patient rhythm, the stove ticking as it cooled, something moving in the hedge outside and going quiet. These were not his sounds, and he did not know any of them, and he lay awake a while learning them, and then he slept, without meaning to, before he had learned them all.

---

The second day was longer than the first. His legs had stiffened in the night and took most of the morning to forgive him. The road climbed out of the farmland into rougher country with fewer walls and more sheep, and long slow hills that looked short until you were on them.

The sheep watched him go by with flat, unimpressed faces. There were no more turnip carts. He walked by himself and watched the milestones, and for nearly half an hour he watched a hawk working a field without ever seeing it catch anything, and by the end he was on its side.

He did not take the leather book out again. He did not need to, because he had read it often enough on the wall that its lines came up by themselves as he walked, like the old descriptions on page seven.

And the record came up by itself too, the green book and the four entries, and this time he made himself do something with it instead of just letting it come.

He did not know their names or how old they had been, or where they had come from, or what they had found themselves able to do before whatever happened to them happened. He did not know whether any of them had had a Hesk, or been given a book. But he knew the one word in the entry that he had read past in the archive, because the other words had been louder.

*Weeks.*

Not years. Not after a long life of trying and failing. Weeks. Whatever had happened to three of them had happened fast and early, before they could have found out what they were, before they could even have found their feet. He thought about the phrase *practice-related*, and about how little practice anyone could do in a few weeks, and about someone, thirty-two years ago, choosing which kind of true to write down.

---

Late in the afternoon, the road began to climb in earnest. It went up a long bare hill in a series of slow bends, with no hedges, only grass and stones and the wind, which had picked up out of the west and was pushing at his back. The sun was low, and his shadow went up the road ahead of him, long and thin, with a bag on its shoulder.

He came over the top and stopped.

Ardenmere lay below him in a wide river valley, and it was enormous.

He had read about it three times in the reference section on the eastern cities. He had known it was larger than Denvash, but he had not known what *larger* meant until he stood on the rise and looked. The river came in from the north, wide and slow and copper-coloured in the low sun, and bent through the middle of the valley like an arm.

On the far bank, in the crook of that arm, rose the Ranked core, walled and stepped, ring inside ring, each ring higher than the last. He could see the districts climbing toward the centre, Copper, he thought, and Iron, and Bronze, and at the very top something white with towers catching the last of the light. He had five pages in a notebook about one wall in Denvash. He could see six walls from here.

And around them, outside them, spilling past every one of them, was the rest.

The Unranked District was not a district. It was a city of its own, running along both banks of the river and up the slopes on either side and out across the flat land to the south. It had no walls at all, and no shape that he could find except the shape of a great many people deciding where to live one at a time.

There was old stone in it, solid and square. There were things that looked built and things that looked stacked, roofs going up on top of roofs, and three or four bridges crossing the river with movement on all of them.

The sun went down while he watched. The gold went out of the river, and the white towers on the Ranked hill held the light a little longer than everything else and then let it go too, and the sky turned grey-gold and then a deep, clear blue.

And the fires came out.

They came out one by one at first and then all at once, cookfires in yards and on roofs and in doorways and along the river, hundreds of them and then more than hundreds, little orange points of light across the whole Unranked District, each with a thin grey thread of smoke going up from it and leaning east in the wind, until the whole valley below him was lit like a field of embers that somebody had banked for the night and would come back to in the morning.

A thousand fires. He could not count them, and for once in his life he did not try. Each one was somebody's dinner, somebody not Ranked enough for the walls, making do.

He stood on the rise with the wind at his back. He was very tired, and his legs ached all the way down. He had a turnip in his bag and a bent bracket in his pocket and a book against his chest that said *I don't know* in his grandfather's handwriting.

He thought about the green book in the archive, with its four entries, three of which had ended in a few weeks and one in a line that said nobody knew. He was the fifth.

On the walk home from the record office he had promised himself there would be no fifth entry in that green book with his name at the top. It had been easy to promise, as things are easy on a walk home. He said it again now, to himself, and it was not easy anymore, because he could see how big the thing was that he would have to say it to.

He took out his notebook, opened it to a clean page, and wrote in the last of the light:

*Three of them were dead within weeks. They didn't get time to find their feet. Whatever happened, it happened before they knew what they were.*

*So that's the first job. Not to find out what [SHATTERED] means. Not yet. First, get past the weeks.*


