   145	A woman came to the wall while they stood there, a solid Copper-equivalent with a good record and a better opinion of it, and asked for a pairing on the next big card against one of the brothers. Cael looked at the board before Dace could speak. There was no cross between her name and the brother's, and no ring beside either. But there were two dots beside the other brother, and none beside her.
   146	
   147	*He'll give the slot to the brother he owes,* Cael thought, *and give her the next card, and she'll be angry.*
   148	
   149	Dace gave the slot to the brother he owed, and promised her the next card, and she was angry, and went away to tell somebody about it.
   150	
   151	"You had that before I opened my mouth," said Dace.
   152	
   153	"I had a guess."
   154	
   155	"Get two more and call it a habit." He took the pencil from behind his ear and turned it over and put it back. "Vell's told me about your rule of three. I'll give you one of mine for nothing, since you're here. This board answers to the people on those benches, and to nobody else. Not a guild, not a district office, not a man in a grey coat with a pin. If the Compact wants to know who fights in Ardenmere, it can pay at the door and sit on a bench and find out the slow way, like everybody else has to."
   156	
   157	He said it lightly, but Cael had seen the corner of Dace's desk through the door of the little room where the door money was counted. On it, under a lump of slag from the old foundry that served as a paperweight, there was a letter on thick cream paper with a printed head. It was the third such letter Cael had seen there in a year. He had never seen one answered. He had seen one used, on a wet evening in the spring, to light the lamp over the north benches when the spills ran out.
   158	
   159	"They write to you," he said.
   160	
   161	"Every season. *A registered venue. Licensed bouts. A modest annual fee, in return for the protection of the Guild's good name.*" Dace said it in a dry high voice, like a clerk reading out a will to people who were not in it. "I've got the Guild's good name. It's under a lump of slag on my desk. It's very useful when the spills run out." He turned back to the slate and squinted at a dot. "The day I take their licence is the day they get a copy of this wall. Not once. Every week, by law, with the rings on. And then a woman who's six weeks out of a cracked wrist is a line in a clerk's book in the Ranked core, and the clerk doesn't care whether she gets put in front of a hard fighter or not." The dot came off under his thumb and went back on, smaller. "So they don't get the wall."
   162	
   163	---
   164	
   165	Stedd lost that afternoon, in the second bout of a thin card, and Cael learned more from the way he did it than from anything Vell had said all day.
   166	
   167	He was a Copper washout of forty or so, soft in the middle and slow on his feet, with a bald patch he made no effort to cover and a laugh like a barrow on cobbles. He had not won in Vell's book since before Cael first walked down the hill. He fought a young Force practitioner who was too quick for him from the first exchange. He went down in the third with his breath knocked out of him, and lay on the stone a moment looking at the beams. Then he got up, slowly, the old way, a hand and a knee and the back last. He shook the young one's hand. And then, without dusting himself off, he walked straight to Vell's table and stood in front of it.
   168	
   169	"Fair," said Stedd.
   170	
   171	That was all. Vell nodded and wrote, and Stedd went to the water barrel.
   172	
   173	And on his way the room nodded to him. Cael watched it happen and could hardly believe it. Fighters on the near benches lifted their chins as he passed. One of Dace's sweepers leaned on her broom to say *well fought*. A Fenrow bookmaker who was famous for acknowledging nobody took one finger off his slate. A man who had just lost in the second bout of a Thursday card crossed the Ironyard like a man coming home with a good harvest.
   174	
   175	"He's not won since before I came," said Cael to Vell, when he could.
   176	
   177	"He's never once told me I was wrong." Vell blotted Stedd's line. "Eleven years of bouts. Win or lose, he walks to this table first, before he's even got his breath, and tells me it was fair. Some of those weren't, as it happens. I've called a few against him I'd call different now." She put the blotter down. "That's what's worth anything in here. You can't win it in a night, and nobody can give it you. You turn up when you said, you fight clean, and you take what I write without making it anybody else's trouble. Keep doing that for eleven years and people will step aside for you at the water barrel. It's the only kind of rich this building recognises."
   178	
   179	He watched for it afterwards, and it was everywhere. The benches nearest Vell's table belonged to the long clean records, whatever their rung, and nobody had ever made it a rule or ever broken it. The bookmakers had the northwest corner and kept to it, and their noise was allowed right up to *begin* and not one breath past it.
   180	
   181	And then, near the end of the afternoon, two strangers came in through the big door together, and he saw the thing he would write down first.
   182	
   183	They were both fighters; he could tell by the way they stood. One wore the stitched shoulders of an academy training coat and boots with thin soles cut for sprung wooden floors. The other wore a patched jacket and the kind of boots you could walk to Fenrow in. They stopped inside the door side by side, and each of them looked, first, at one thing.
   184	
   185	The guild boy looked at the slate, at the names, at how big the chalk was and whose it was.
   186	
   187	The circuit girl looked at Vell's table, to see whether Vell was at it, and what she was writing.
   188	
   189	It took less than a breath. Neither of them knew they had done it. And there it was, the whole difference between the two worlds he lived between, in one turn of two heads: one of them walking in to see who could be beaten, and the other walking in to see who was keeping the account.
   190	
   191	He wrote it all down that evening at the crate desk, in the grey book and not the slim one, because none of it was about him. The fixed points on the pine board, and the strings that hung from them, and the small pencilled number for how far out a rung hung. The red rules and *t.f.* The rule, and the three stopped bouts, and the quiet body. Dace's rings and dots and stitches, kept in chalk because chalk wipes. Stedd's *fair*. The two heads turning at the door.
   192	
   193	Then, under it all, he wrote something that was not a fact, and boxed it, so that he would know later that it was only what he thought.
   194	
   195	*Nobody made them build it honest. People the registry wouldn't let in built this out of nothing but a book and a slate, and they could have built it to sell. Every day somebody in that building chooses not to, and that's all that holds it up. The record is only as straight as the hand that keeps it.*
   196	
   197	He looked at it for a while, and then put a small star beside the box, the first star in the new book, and sat with the lamp until the oil began to smell.

