     1	# Chapter 3 — Two Coats
     2	
     3	There was a page near the middle of the grey book that had no fighters on it at all.
     4	
     5	He had started it in the old Log, in his first autumn, after a man in a grey coat with a pin in his collar had walked the market one afternoon from end to end asking about lodgers without papers. When the old Log filled, the page had come across into the new book with everything else that was still alive, and it had grown. At the top, in capitals, it said *OFFICIALS*. Under that it was a list, kept in the same three columns as everything else, of what he had seen of the Compact in Ardenmere with his own eyes. Under the seen things went what he had only been told, with a small *h* beside each of those.
     6	
     7	There was the pin man, and the dark coat off the west cart who had asked for him by his whole name. There was Coss, who had sat across a pie from him and told him about a daughter. There was the woman Ilsev, at a table, with a little black book with a strap. And under them, as the months went on, there were the sweeps.
     8	
     9	They had come through the district a few times a season when he first arrived, and nobody had paid them much mind. Then, half a year ago, they had begun to come more often, and nobody in the district could say why, and everybody in the district had an opinion. He had written down the dates. Some months there were three. One month there had been five. He had looked at the column of dates for a long time one evening and then made himself stop looking. There was a thing he wanted to write beside it and he had no evidence for it at all, and a thing with no evidence did not go on the page, however loud it was in his head.
    10	
    11	What did go on the page was how they moved. *Always two. One of them writes. Better boots on the one who doesn't. Mid-morning, never early, never late.* He had seen four sweeps whole, from start to finish, and parts of several more, and each one had walked the district as if it were following a line painted on the cobbles that only the officers could see.
    12	
    13	It was a quiet morning when the fifth came. He and Lira were in their alcove at the north end of the Ironyard. The straw post stood in its corner; she had brought it down from the Cinder House on a borrowed handcart, with its grey canvas and its chalk columns and Red Cap's fist still printed in the straw at the height of a small boy's shoulder. They were doing slow work, because of the thigh. She would come at him at half speed with the staff, and he would move his feet, and she would tell him which of his feet had been wrong, which was usually both.
    14	
    15	Red Cap came in at the big door at a run, saw them, and stopped running, which was how Cael knew before he said anything.
    16	
    17	He came across the floor at a walk. He was very bad at walking when he wanted to run; he did it with his arms stiff, like a boy carrying two full buckets. At the alcove he leaned on the post and said, to nobody, in his ordinary voice, "Two coats at the river gate."
    18	
    19	"How long?" said Lira.
    20	
    21	"Just now. Coming up the tannery lane." He was already turning to go, because he had other people to tell. "Pie boy's gone for Dace."
    22	
    23	Lira looked at Cael. She did not say anything. She did not need to; they had talked about this, in the abstract, a dozen times, in the evenings with the door open between their rooms. She had always said the same thing: the worst thing he could do on a sweep morning was look like a person who had something to do on a sweep morning.
    24	
    25	"I'm going to watch," he said.
    26	
    27	"From where?"
    28	
    29	"From where they'd expect a boy to be." He picked up his coat from the hook. "The market. Then home."
    30	
    31	She held his eyes a moment, measuring. Then she nodded, and put the staff on her shoulder, and turned toward the empty floor of the alcove. "I'll be here, making a noise," she said. "Dace likes a noise on sweep days. It sounds like nothing's wrong."
    32	
    33	---
    34	
    35	He went down to the market by the dyers' steps, at a stroll, and sat down on the bottom stair beside the fruit woman's baskets with a bag of dried pears he had not needed, and waited.
    36	
    37	The fruit woman did not look at him. She said, to the far end of the row, "You're early for your pears."
    38	
    39	"I'm late for my pears. I bought them yesterday."
    40	
    41	"Then you're sitting here eating yesterday's pears on my step." She sniffed. "Suit yourself. You're no worse than a cat."
    42	
    43	The market had not changed, and it had changed completely. The stalls were all open; nobody had pulled a shutter or a cloth. That was the first rule of a sweep morning, the one every stallholder in the east lanes knew in their bones and would never have said aloud: you did not close, because closing was a thing the procedure could write down. But the market had gone quiet in a way he could hear. Prices were still being called, but nobody was calling them very loud. The bearded fish cousin, who on an ordinary morning would talk a widow into three fish she did not want, was selling one fish at a time, for the exact coin, and putting it straight into the hand without the usual argument. At the paper stall the man with ink on his cuffs had taken the account book off his counter and put it under the counter. The baker had stopped giving anyone credit, even the fighters she gave credit to every week of their lives, and the fighters had stopped asking.
    44	
    45	Nothing was hidden. Everything was simply small.
    46	
    47	And the children had gone.
    48	
    49	He noticed it the way you notice a clock has stopped: not at the moment, but a while after, when the silence has been going on long enough to have a shape. On an ordinary morning there were children everywhere in the market, under the stalls and on the steps and darting between legs with messages and stolen apricots. Now there were none. The lane at the top of the row, which he could see from the step, was as empty as a church on a Monday.
    50	
    51	Then the two coats came round the corner by the well, and he saw why. Half a street behind them, peering out from the mouth of an alley they had just passed, were three of the smallest boys from the east lanes, who had vanished in front of the officers and reappeared, as if by magic, the moment they had gone by.
    52	
    53	The officers walked side by side, at the same steady pace, as if they were measuring the street. Both wore the long grey coat with the collar pin, a little silver mark that caught the light. The one on the left was younger and thinner and carried a flat leather case in his left hand and a pencil in his right. The one on the right was older and walked half a pace slower and carried nothing at all, and his boots were better. Cael looked at the boots first, because the boots told you which of two officials you had to watch; the one with good boots did not take notes, because somebody took them for him.
    54	
    55	They came down the row. The left-hand one looked at each stall as they passed it, and sometimes wrote, and once asked the bearded cousin a question Cael could not hear. The bearded cousin answered it with his whole face, shaking his head and pointing back at the river as if it were the river's fault. The right-hand one looked at nothing in particular, and therefore at everything.
    56	
    57	When they reached the bottom of the steps, the right-hand one's eyes passed over the fruit woman, and her baskets, and the boy beside her eating pears.
    58	
    59	They did not stop. Why would they? A boy with a limp, on a market step, on a weekday, eating fruit. Cael let the look pass over him as he would let a breeze go over him, and went on chewing, and counted his breath while the look was on him, and it was four breaths, which was not long. He was surprised to find his hands were steady.
    60	
    61	"Are they gone?" said the fruit woman, without moving her eyes from the far end of the row.
    62	
    63	"Going. Toward the boards."
    64	
    65	"Then pass me the pears," she said, "since you're not eating them."
    66	
    67	He passed her the pears, and she ate one, and he got up and went the other way, by the back lane behind the paper stall, at the same stroll he had come down at.
    68	
    69	---
    70	
    71	He knew where they were going next because they always went there next.
    72	
    73	That was the strangest thing on the officials page, and the most useful. Every sweep he had ever seen had walked the same road: in at the river gate, up the tannery lane, down the market row, along to the three notice boards at the triangle where they read every card and every notice and wrote some of them down. Then they went across to the boarding-house row behind the Ironyard, where most of the district's fighters lived, and out by the north gate. The road never changed. It did not change when the north gate was half blocked by a brewer's dray, or when the market row was flooded with a burst gutter; the officers simply walked through the dray's horses and the flood. The road had been written down somewhere, by someone in a room a long way from Ardenmere, and the people who walked it did not know there were four better streets.
    74	
    75	So he went home by the back lanes and got there first, and went up the narrow stairs to the two back rooms, and did not go to the window at the back. He went to the small window on the landing at the front, which looked down the length of the row from the end. He had found it in his first week in the house and had sat there with his book a hundred evenings since. If anybody looked up from the row, they would see what they always saw, which was a boy on a landing with a book.
    76	
    77	The heavyset man was on his front step, as he always was. He had his arms folded on his chest and his hat pushed back. He was watching the end of the row with exactly the face he used for weather.
    78	
    79	The coats came round the corner a quarter of an hour later.
    80	
    81	They worked down the row house by house. At each door they stopped, and knocked, or did not need to because somebody was already on the step, and the young one opened his case and asked a question, and wrote. At two doors they went in for a little while and came out again. At the house next but one to Cael's, a woman came out with a baby on her hip and talked very fast and showed them a paper, and they read it and gave it back and moved on.
    82	
    83	Then they were at his step, and the heavyset man unfolded his arms.
    84	
    85	Cael could not hear what was said. He did not try to. He kept his eyes on the page in front of him and let the edges of his sight do the work, as he did at the rope when he wanted to watch a fighter without the fighter knowing it. The heavyset man went in and came out again with the lodgers' book, which lived on a shelf inside the door, and held it out open. The young officer took it. He turned one page back, and ran his pencil down it, line by line, and Cael knew to the line where his own name was, because he had signed it there himself on the day they moved in: *C. Hesk-ward, back first floor, two rooms with L.*
    86	
    87	Everything he had was on that page. If the pencil stopped at his line, the heavyset man would be asked questions he could not answer, and a landlord who has been asked questions about a lodger once does not want to be asked them twice. If they knocked and took him for a talk at the district office, he would miss the card, and a fighter who missed a card without a reason found his name rubbed off Dace's wall. Two rooms, a door between them that stood open by day, a name at the start of a line in Vell's book: all of it hung, this minute, on the speed of one man's pencil.
    88	
    89	The pencil went down the page at the same speed all the way. It did not slow. It did not stop at his name, or at Lira's, or at anybody's. It reached the bottom of the page and the young officer gave the book back.
    90	
    91	The one with good boots said something to the heavyset man. The heavyset man answered with three words and a shrug. Then the two coats went on to the next door, and the heavyset man stood on his step with the book under his arm and watched them all the way to the far end of the row. Only when they had turned the corner toward the north gate did he go inside.
    92	
    93	Cael sat on the landing with the book open on his knee and found he had read the same line eleven times.
    94	
    95	He had not hidden. That was the thing he kept turning over. He had been exactly where the lodgers' book said he lived, on the morning the procedure came to read the lodgers' book, and the procedure had read it and gone away satisfied, because everything was where a procedure would expect it to be. Suppose he had been anywhere else: on the cooper's roof, or by the river, or at the far end of the district. Then there would have been an empty room behind a name in a book, and an empty room was a question. A boy on a landing with a book was not a question. A boy on a landing with a book was nothing at all.
    96	
    97	---
    98	
    99	When he came down the hill to the Ironyard, an hour after the coats had gone out through the north gate, the slate wall by the big door was black from end to end.
   100	
   101	Not a name on it. Not a ring or a dot or a stitch. Somebody had wiped it with a wet cloth and then with a dry one, so that it shone. The only mark left on the whole wall was a long pale smear near the bottom where a hand had leaned while the cloth went round.
   102	
   103	Dace was standing in front of it with a stick of chalk in each hand.
   104	
   105	He did not look round when Cael came up beside him. He was writing already, fast, in white: the next card first, then the one after, the names going up in their columns at the speed of a man writing out a list he has known since he was a boy. He did not stop to think. He did not look at anything but the slate. Now and then he changed hands, and the yellow chalk went on, and the rings began to appear beside names in yellow, and then the dots.
   106	
   107	Cael took out the grey book and turned to the back. He had copied the whole board into it two days before, every name and mark, as an exercise, to see how much of it he could read on his own. He held the page open below the level of his elbow and watched Dace's hand and checked it.
   108	
   109	The names came back in their exact places. The rings came back beside the same names, the Stone woman from the tannery lanes, a Blade from the river end. The stitches went back between the brothers, and between the two who owed each other money. The dots came back, one and two.
   110	
   111	Then Dace put a ring beside a name that Cael's copy had no ring beside at all, a young Shield fighter who had won on Tuesday and walked out of the building, Cael would have sworn, on two good legs.
   112	
   113	He looked at the name in his book, bare. He looked at the ring on the wall.
   114	
   115	"He hurt his back," said Dace, without turning round, "lifting his mother's mangle on Wednesday. He told me on Thursday. Nobody else knows, and nobody else is going to, and you didn't see me write that."
   116	
   117	"I didn't see anything."
   118	
   119	"Good. That's the right answer, all day today." Dace stepped back and looked at the whole wall, and nodded at it, once, the way the heavyset man had nodded at the end of the row. It had taken him a little under the hour. "They read the boards at the triangle. They always do. Every card I put up there is in a case in a grey coat by now, and it's welcome to be. That's the public part. It's meant to be read." He tapped the slate with his knuckle. "This isn't public. This is who's hurt and who's owed and whose mother can't watch them fight each other. They can have the cards. They can't have this."
   120	
   121	"They didn't come in here."
   122	
   123	"They never have. Not in twelve years." Dace put the chalk in his pocket, and dusted his hands, and for a moment, looking at the wall, he looked older and more tired than Cael had ever seen him. "That's six months of this, now. Four last month. They didn't use to come four times in a year." He shook his head. "I don't know what they want. I don't think they know. I think somebody in a room somewhere has written *more often* on a paper, and two men in grey coats are walking it."
   124	
   125	"How do you hold it?" said Cael. "All of it. I copied the whole wall two days ago, sitting down, with a pencil, and I still didn't have the mangle."
   126	
   127	Dace looked at him sidelong, as if deciding whether the question was worth the breath, and seemed to decide that it was.
   128	
   129	"Faces," he said. "I can't keep a list in my head any better than you can. Nobody can. But I can keep a room." He nodded at the benches, empty now in the grey light from the high windows. "The Shield boy told me about his mother's mangle sitting on the third bench from the barrel, with his elbows on his knees, looking at the floor because he was ashamed of it. So I put the ring on the wall, and I put the ring on him, on that bench, in my head. When I want it back, I go and look at the bench." He shrugged. "The whole wall's in this room somewhere, if you know where people sat when they told you things. I've been sitting people down on those benches for twelve years."
   130	
   131	Cael looked at the empty benches and tried to see it, and could not. Then for a moment he could. There was the Stone woman with her wrapped wrist on the near end of the second row. There were the two brothers who were never, he realised, on the same side of the floor at once, and their mother between them with her apples. It went again almost at once. But he had seen it, and he understood that the slate was only where Dace kept the copy.
   132	
   133	He went off toward Vell's table. Cael stood a while longer in front of the wall, with its names restored and its rings and dots and stitches, and the smear near the bottom where a hand had leaned, which Dace had not bothered to rub out.
   134	
   135	*Chalk wipes,* he thought. Last week it had sounded like a joke.
   136	
   137	---
   138	
   139	He wrote it up that evening, on the officials page, with Lira sitting on the end of his bed eating bread and reading over his shoulder. She was allowed to with this page, because there was nothing on it that was his.
   140	
   141	*Fifth sweep watched whole. Two coats, left one young, writes, case in left hand, pencil in right. Right one older, better boots, carries nothing, looks at nothing, sees everything. Mid-morning. River gate, tannery lane, market, triangle, the row, north gate. Same road as every other time. The road doesn't bend for drays or water.*
   142	
   143	*The district: nothing shut. Everything small. Exact coin, ledgers under counters, no credit. Children: none on the road in front of them, the whole way; three behind, out of an alley the moment the coats had passed. Board at the Ironyard wiped before they reached the triangle, rebuilt from Dace's head inside an hour. One ring he had and I didn't (a mangle, h).*
   144	
   145	*The row: the lodgers' book on the step. Read one page, top to bottom, same speed. Didn't stop at mine. Nothing written that I saw.*
   146	
   147	"You were on the landing," said Lira. "With a book."
   148	
   149	"With a book."
   150	
   151	"I'd have been on the cooper's roof." She chewed. "Or under the bed."
   152	
   153	"I nearly went to the roof." He turned the pencil over. "Then I thought about what they'd have seen if they'd looked up at the window and there wasn't anybody in it. Or if they'd asked the man on the step where the two at the back were this morning, and he'd had to say he didn't know."
   154	
   155	Lira stopped chewing.
   156	
   157	"He looked in," she said. "The young one. On the way past the big door. I was making a noise on the post the way Dace likes, and he stopped in the door and looked at me for about as long as it takes to say *good morning*, and then he went on." She put the bread down. "He looked at me like I was weather. Like I was a girl hitting a sack, which I was." She was quiet a moment. "I'm in their book too, you know. Same as you. *Wind, Copper*, and under it whatever Fenmark wrote when they sent me home. I've never seen it. I don't want to." She picked the bread up again and did not eat it. "When I first came down from the academy I couldn't walk past a collar pin without my hands going cold. For a whole winter. I used to cross the street."
   158	
   159	"You didn't cross the street this morning."
   160	
   161	"No," said Lira, and looked surprised, as if she had only just noticed. "No, I hit the post."
   162	
   163	He wrote the last part slowly, because it was the part that mattered, and he wanted to get it right in case he needed it some morning when he was frightened.
   164	
   165	*They don't find you by looking for you. They find you by looking where the paper says you are and seeing whether you're there. So be there. Be exactly where the paper says, doing exactly what a person would be doing, on the day they come to read the paper. Don't be hidden. Be findable, but only where they look, and only as much as they look for.*
   166	
   167	He underlined *only where they look*.
   168	
   169	"That's horrible," said Lira, after a while. "It's also right. That's the worst kind." She tore her bread in two and gave him half without looking at him. "Does it bother you, being in their book? Your name on that page with his pencil going down it?"
   170	
   171	"Everybody's in their book," said Cael. "That's what the book's for." He looked at the line on the page about the pencil, which had gone down at the same speed all the way. "What I want to know is whether my line's in some other book as well. One I've never seen. And I can't find that out by looking out of a window."
   172	
   173	She did not say anything. She sat on the end of the bed beside him in the lamplight, and ate her half of the bread. After a while she leaned her shoulder against his, as she did when she had no answer and did not intend to pretend she had one.
     1	# Chapter 4 — The Keepers
     2	
     3	The man who wanted to argue with Vell sat on the bench nearest Dace's slate for most of an hour before he did it, and Cael, who had come in to watch the morning card's first bout, ended up watching him instead.
     4	
