   151	Cael remembered him then. The third bench back from the east wall, a river-academy coat, both hands flat on his knees, and his eyes on the stone where the landing had been, not on the man who had fallen. He had seen it and filed it nowhere.
   152	
   153	He stood in front of the wall for a while.
   154	
   155	He thought about the half-breath at the end of every burst, and Lira's sentence, and the people on the benches who were not there for the fun of it. Somebody was going to wait for the landing. He had known it since the spring. He had spent ten trials and two days of a hollow chest finding out what the landing was worth, and he had a rule now, and a half-breath that was no longer only a wound. He had not yet once spent it on purpose against anybody but Lira. He did not know what it was worth with a stranger's strike in it.
   156	
   157	"Put me on," he said.
   158	
   159	"Third slot. Next week." Dace wrote *Hesk-ward* in the space, in white, and drew a small mark beside it that Cael had never seen on the wall before, a short line with a dot under it. "That's for me," said Dace, before Cael could ask. "It means *watch this one*. Not you. Him." He put the chalk in his pocket. "Vell's got something for you, too. She was asking where you were."
   160	
   161	Vell was at her table with the day's book shut and a letter open on top of it. She did not hand it to him. She turned it a little toward him, the way she turned the ledger, so that he could see it was a letter. He could see the hand, a slanting brown hand he did not know, and the place it came from, which was a river town two days downstream.
   162	
   163	"A keeper," said Vell. "Down at the salt end. I've never met her. She keeps a small book at a bad yard." She tapped the letter. "She's had three fighters through in a month who'd been up here, and all three of them told her about a boy in my book with *atypical* written beside his name more often than anybody's she's ever heard of. She wants to know what I mean by it." Vell folded the letter in three. "She's not the first. She's the first who's written. Keepers talk, as I told that guild boy, and they don't only talk about complaints."
   164	
   165	"What will you tell her?"
   166	
   167	"What I always tell anybody who asks me what a word in my book means." Vell put the letter in the pocket of her coat. "Come and see."
   168	
   169	She sat back, and he thought she had finished. Then she reached for the day's book and opened it, not at the day's page but at the very back, at the inside of the back cover, where the board was bare except for a few lines in pencil. She turned it so that he could see one of them and kept her hand over the rest.
   170	
   171	It was a single word, *Atypical*, with a row of small upright strokes beside it, the kind you make when you are counting something you do not want to lose count of. There were a great many strokes.
   172	
   173	"I've kept that for thirty years," said Vell. "Every time I write that word against a name, I put a stroke there. Eleven, in all the years before you walked down the hill." She took her hand off the page and let him count, and did not wait for him to finish. "You can see what's happened since."
   174	
   175	He could. The strokes went on past the eleven in a steady row, and then in a second row under the first, in five-bar gates, the way Hesk counted bearings.
   176	
   177	"I didn't write it against you, the day after Darrow," she said. "It looked too small. But I've written it every time since, because it's the only word I've got that's true and doesn't pretend to know more than I do." She closed the book. "If a keeper at the salt end wants to know what I mean by it, the only honest answer is that I don't know either, and I'd like somebody to. So, come and see." She looked at him with her flat thorough look. "I don't suppose you'll tell her."
   178	
   179	"I don't know either."
   180	
   181	"No," said Vell. "I didn't think you did. I'm only keeping count."
   182	
   183	---
   184	
   185	He came back from the east bench late that night, past the end of the card, past the time the sweepers finished, when the Ironyard should have been dark.
   186	
   187	It was not dark. As he came down the lane toward the boarding-house row, he saw a light in one of the high windows along the north wall, the one above the alcoves at that end. It was a small light, not a lamp from the main floor. Somebody had lit a single practice lamp on a hook and was working by it.
   188	
   189	He stopped in the lane.
   190	
   191	The side door at the north end was off the latch; it always was, if you knew how to lift it. He did not go to it. He stood in the dark of the lane, across from the window, and listened, and after a moment he heard the sound he had heard every morning for a year: the dry scuff of feet on stone, and then nothing, and then the scuff again, further along. The step. The floor going short under somebody, over and over. And then, after the step, something he did not know. A second sound, close behind the first, as though whoever was making it had landed and not stopped, and gone on, and landed again.
   192	
   193	He knew who it was. He knew the rhythm of her feet better than he knew his own handwriting.
   194	
   195	She had said *an hour* when she went out, as she said most evenings, with her staff over her shoulder. It was past the middle of the night.
   196	
   197	He stood there a long time. He thought about Vell's book, where her line said *Copper-equivalent, high range*, and about the girls from the wall, whom she taught to fall for a copper each. He thought about her dock partner, who went home when his hour was done. He thought about a girl in a barn copying a left-handed man for a year before anybody at an academy ever looked at her. He thought about an academy that had looked at her for as long as it cared to and then sent her home. He thought about her face on the bench in the alcove when she had said *I'll tell you when it works*, and the thing that had gone across it too fast for him to read.
   198	
