   200	
   201	He did not go in. Whatever she was building on the straw at this hour, by one lamp, she was building it because she had decided to, and she would bring it to him when it was ready to be looked at, or before, if she chose. It was hers to bring. He would not take it off her by walking in on it.
   202	
   203	But he would not pretend, either, that he had not seen.
   204	
   205	He went home, and up the narrow stairs, and into his room, and did not light the lamp. He sat on the end of his bed in the dark with his coat still on, and listened to the house: the heavyset man's slow tread on the floor below, locking up; somebody's kettle; the street going quiet. The door between the two rooms stood open on her empty room.
   206	
   207	After a long while he heard the street door, and the heavyset man say something low, and her feet on the stairs, two at a time, quiet. She came along the passage. He heard her stop outside her own door, and stand there, perhaps seeing his door open and his room dark, perhaps not. Then she went in and sat down on her bed, and he heard her let out a long breath, the kind you let out at the end of a thing that has cost you more than you meant to pay.
