editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-06.md:73:*A few years*, in the side room. *Some years*, under the lamps on Chandler Street, about the folding chairs. They were the same span of time. For as long as Hesk had known what Cael's coupling looked like, he had been going to sit in the dark behind a curtain every Tuesday and learn how a body moves when it carries a Path, so that he would know what he was seeing if it ever happened to Cael's. Then, on the night before Kindling, with nothing left to learn and no time left to explain it, he had taken Cael along. He had sat him down and said *watch his hips*, and *the third exchange is where they tell you the truth*, and *there*, out loud, over and over, so that one day, with Hesk a week's post away and no chair beside him in the dark, Cael would be able to say *there* for himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-06.md:211:Not years. Not after a long life of trying and failing. Weeks. Whatever had happened to three of them had happened fast and early, before they could have found out what they were, before they could even have found their feet. He thought about the phrase *practice-related*, and about how little practice anyone could do in a few weeks, and about someone, thirty-two years ago, choosing which kind of true to write down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-06.md:247:*Three of them were dead within weeks. They didn't get time to find their feet. Whatever happened, it happened before they knew what they were.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:3:The day after Kindling was the strangest day of all, because nothing happened in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:129:*Of the four (4) recorded instances, disposition is as follows: three (3) practitioners are recorded as deceased within weeks of classification, cause of death listed as "practice-related incident" in Compact records. One (1) practitioner's disposition is recorded as unknown, last registry update dated 32 years prior to the present record.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:133:He had known the number four as you know a fact you read once and set on a shelf, and until this moment he had never turned it into people. Now he did. Four people with names, who had each stepped out of a circle somewhere and heard what he had heard, and three of them had been dead within weeks. One was not accounted for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:161:"Yes." Cael sat on the stool across the bench from him, the stool he had sat on since his feet did not reach the floor; they reached now. "Three of them died. One, nobody knows."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:179:Hesk looked at the housing, and then back at Cael. "The fourth record, the one with no disposition. Thirty-two years ago." He paused and picked the next words out one by one, like a man who had practised them for years without ever knowing which day he would need them. "I was twenty-two, serving two years in the Compact's regional logistics division. No fighting. I routed supplies, which is the work they hand to steady Iron-tiers who keep their questions to themselves. That file was opened while I was there. I watched it begin. I never saw how it finished."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:211:The third was the current one, with eleven descriptions of Kindling and two stars and a Stone Path declaration in Joren's round hand near the back. Folded into its back pages was a copy of a registry entry about four people he had never met and was never going to stop thinking about. Into the bag they went in order, the oldest underneath, as you stack a thing you mean to keep adding to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-59.md:165:He folded the letter along its own folds and put it in the front of the Log, inside the cover, on top of the other two. *You're describing a home. It's not nothing. I'm proud of you.* The three of them lay together there, a little thicker each time, and the cover did not quite close over them any more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-12.md:141:"There's a Blade called Renn who keeps at me for work," Vell said, still writing. "Copper Rank 3. This season he's had eleven bouts and lost three of them. For an unrated debut tonight, he's what I have. Anybody easier isn't fighting, and nobody who's fighting is easier. He'll not go gentle on you for your age. That's not how this yard works, and you'd not thank me if it were. Somebody goes soft on you the first time, you learn something about this place that isn't so, and you pay for it later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:67:Pellin found the file, checked a line with her pen, and made a small mark. "Kindling assessment, the usual way. Your Arbiter wakes here, and the result goes straight to the registry ledger." She laid two fingers on a flat brass plate let into the desktop at her elbow, dull and unremarkable, about the size of a book. "When your result reaches the Registry, this lights, and I record what it shows. The resolution itself is usually done before you've finished a breath; with the paperwork, the whole appointment runs from three minutes to fifteen. Do you have questions first?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:81:A year of being ready, and he was not ready, and it turned out you could be both. He knew the procedural summary by heart: *The practitioner stands within the marked circle. No external device is required. Every practitioner's Arbiter is their own, dormant since birth, and Kindling is the moment it wakes.* What the waking felt like, nobody had managed to tell him; only that it came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:169:He was fourteen. A minute ago, perhaps, he had been given a classification the Registry's own summary called deprecated and a safety risk to the practitioner, held by four people in its whole history. He tried to feel something about that and could not find the place where the feeling should go. Instead he looked at the tree and thought, *that's what it is.* Not the word. The tree. Something that had set off one way and was now testing whether there was another way to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:261:Cael thought about the notebooks, three of them, full of things he had never decided to start writing down. He thought about the hall last night, when he had said *there*, out loud, half a second before the strike, without knowing how he knew. He thought about Joren's heel coming up at the end of a sentence and his own two fingers already moving before he had finished thinking *now*. But that was only watching, and he had always watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:289:He did not know how much she knew. Kindling results travelled through a neighbourhood like water through a ditch, so she probably knew that his had not gone well, and probably not the word. What she had given him was what he had given her on the step: notice, and nothing asked in return. That evening, under her starred line on page seven, he wrote in small letters: *She was right that it's done. It doesn't feel done.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:15:He had shared a desk that year with a man who was. The man saw patterns everywhere: in the dates on a run of permits, in which way a witness looked when she lied, in the order a family hung its washing. About a third of the patterns were real. The other two thirds were the man's own cleverness, reflected back at him off things that meant nothing, and he could not tell the thirds apart, because to him they all shone the same. He had gone on finding them, faster and faster, until a report of his sent four agents into a valley after a smuggling ring that was only three farmers who did not like each other. After that he had been moved to a records office in a town on the coast, where he copied other men's reports into a ledger, and was not unhappy, and wrote a very fine hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:23:The first sheet was a copy of the registration. *Caelen Hesk-ward. Registration 41-7843-V.* The certifying office was on a street called Weaver's Row, in Denvash, and the officer was a Warden-Adjunct named Pellin, whose signature was small and quick and gave nothing away. The date of the Kindling was in the autumn, fifty-odd days ago. On the line for the classification there was one word in square brackets, stamped, and no tier, no rank and no Path.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:27:The third sheet was a gate entry. *Denvash, Outer gate, eastbound, on foot.* It was dated the third day after the Kindling. It was passive, which meant that a guard had read the card in the ordinary way and the record had marked it and nobody had been asked to do anything about it. Eastbound from Denvash, on foot, was the merchant road, and the merchant road came here in two days, and anybody who knew the road would have guessed this city. That part was not strange.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-28.md:41:A non-standard result at Kindling set a procedure going. He had never handled a [SHATTERED] file himself; he did not know anybody who had. But he had handled three files for results the standard framework had trouble with, and they had all grown the same way, in the same order, as faithfully as a tree grows rings. The certifying district wrote a preliminary risk assessment within the first week, because the certifying district was frightened of being blamed for anything that came after. Then a residential compliance note followed, setting out what the household's standing now was and what it was likely to become. Then there was a cross-reference to the standing-holder's own file, because the residential notice threatened a guild review in so many words. When the Compact notified a guild under that statute, the guild opened its review by its own procedure, and the review had a number, and the number went into the file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-26.md:167:He laid the old notebook open on the blanket beside the Log, and the torn page between them, and looked at the three of them in a row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-18.md:299:*Copper. Rank 2. Wind Path.* Her two declarations underneath, in the order they had come, the first one from Kindling and the second from the spring, which had come with its own odd small rush of pleasure that she still remembered. There was no new line and no change. It had looked exactly like this since the spring, and she was used to it, and only very occasionally did it annoy her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-18.md:305:She had never asked him. You did not ask; declarations were private, and an Arbiter was the most private thing a person had, and she had not asked anybody what their Arbiter said since the week of her own Kindling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-06.md:73:*A few years*, in the side room. *Some years*, under the lamps on Chandler Street, about the folding chairs. They were the same span of time. For as long as Hesk had known what Cael's coupling looked like, he had been going to sit in the dark behind a curtain every Tuesday and learn how a body moves when it carries a Path, so that he would know what he was seeing if it ever happened to Cael's. Then, on the night before Kindling, with nothing left to learn and no time left to explain it, he had taken Cael along. He had sat him down and said *watch his hips*, and *the third exchange is where they tell you the truth*, and *there*, out loud, over and over, so that one day, with Hesk a week's post away and no chair beside him in the dark, Cael would be able to say *there* for himself.
