editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-06.md:247:*Three of them were dead within weeks. They didn't get time to find their feet. Whatever happened, it happened before they knew what they were.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-39.md:61:He tried to see it, Hesk sitting on the step of the workshop on Fen Street, in the street, where people could see him, with a letter in his hands and his big square face doing something Cael had never once seen it do, and he could not. He had seen Hesk prepared, and still, and careful, and sometimes angry, and once, across the workshop bench on the second day after Kindling, ashamed, but he had never seen him afraid. It had happened anyway, a week ago, on a step, where nobody but strangers could see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-45.md:203:*I don't know what it is either. The archive tells you what became of four people, as far as anybody wrote it down. It doesn't tell you what any of them ever did. Don't go looking in it for an answer it hasn't got.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-45.md:255:He lay on his right side on the cot with Yeni's lamp down to a bead and the wheezing man whistling by the door, and he did the thing he had done on the night before Kindling and the night before Renn and every night before a bout since: he went through it. Not the fight, which he did not know, but everything round it, the things he could count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-45.md:267:He woke before the light, as he had woken on the morning of Kindling, and lay still and took stock, as he did on days that mattered: ceiling, the beam across it with its old nail holes, the wheezing man asleep, Yeni's cot empty already, the grey of an Ardenmere morning in the window, which he had stopped seeing months ago and saw again now on purpose. He got up and dressed and went down, and Torvin's wife put a bowl of oats in front of him without a word, and stood over him with her spoon until he had eaten all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-49.md:101:"Section one also says what a primary classification is. *The classification returned by the practitioner's Arbiter at Kindling and entered in the registry, including any entry made in the Supplementary Register.*" He did not hurry. "Mine was returned at Kindling. It took eleven seconds. It's entered in the Supplementary Register, statute nineteen, with an incidence count. I have a primary classification. It isn't one the standard framework can use, which is why section four's first half fits me. But section four lets you enter a practitioner into the category, and section one says who the category is for. It's for practitioners without one. Not being assessable by ordinary means isn't the same as not being classified."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-49.md:137:"You're right. The temporary category doesn't reach you. I've entered eleven people under section four in fifteen years, and every one of them came to me because their Arbiter returned nothing at all, or returned something nobody wrote down. I have never had a practitioner across this desk with a recorded classification that the framework couldn't use." She was quiet a moment. "The regulation wasn't written by anybody who expected you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:3:The day after Kindling was the strangest day of all, because nothing happened in it.
