editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:161:"Yes." Cael sat on the stool across the bench from him, the stool he had sat on since his feet did not reach the floor; they reached now. "Three of them died. One, nobody knows."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:179:Hesk looked at the housing, and then back at Cael. "The fourth record, the one with no disposition. Thirty-two years ago." He paused and picked the next words out one by one, like a man who had practised them for years without ever knowing which day he would need them. "I was twenty-two, serving two years in the Compact's regional logistics division. No fighting. I routed supplies, which is the work they hand to steady Iron-tiers who keep their questions to themselves. That file was opened while I was there. I watched it begin. I never saw how it finished."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:211:The third was the current one, with eleven descriptions of Kindling and two stars and a Stone Path declaration in Joren's round hand near the back. Folded into its back pages was a copy of a registry entry about four people he had never met and was never going to stop thinking about. Into the bag they went in order, the oldest underneath, as you stack a thing you mean to keep adding to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:253:It came from the place where the sigil had gathered on the morning of his Kindling, small and risen, before it spoke one word and went out. That place had been dark ever since; he had gone looking for it in Torvin's yard on the twenty-eighth night, for an hour, and found only the place where a door should have been. He could feel that it was still dark. Nothing lit. No sigil came up out of it to the edge of his sight. But in the dark, the way words come up on the inside of your eyelids when your eyelids are open, something was written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-29.md:139:He did not decide. He had no evidence that would let him decide. He drew a short line under the three of them, and under it wrote: *Don't guess. Don't change what I do. Tell Lira.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-42.md:121:He told her about the quiet before a thought. He told her about the dark place behind his breastbone where the sigil had gathered at Kindling, and had been dark ever since, and how it had still been dark, and how something had been written in it anyway. He opened the Log to the back, to the page where he had copied it, and turned it round on his knee so that she could read it, and she leaned forward on the bed and read it with her lips moving very slightly, as she read the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-30.md:31:So he took a summons form out of the case, where three of them lay flat under the six sheets, the only weight the case had carried, and filled it in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-30.md:163:He thought about Hesk on the roof at Fen Street, with the grey dog crossing below and the tea going cold, saying *there are provisions*. He thought about the green book in the archive in Denvash and its three entries, dead within weeks, and the fourth, unknown, and the phrase *practice-related incident*, and somebody, long ago, choosing which kind of true to write down. He thought about a grey coat walking the market slowly from one end to the other, and a dark coat on a doorstep asking for his whole name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-30.md:197:"Third lane past the well, going up," said Yeni. "The tall house with the rain barrel that's lost its hoop. She's in the roof, with the two sisters from the dye sheds. Don't knock the front. The woman who owns it is deaf in one ear and cross in both." She lay down again. "Go round the side. There's a window at the bottom of the roof stair. Knock on the glass, three times, soft. The sisters sleep like the dead and Lira doesn't sleep like anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-41.md:27:*Not taking. It isn't all there, and it isn't mine the way Lira's Path is hers. She was declared at Kindling and it's been hers every day since, whole, to use when she likes. What I've got is more like the outline of how she moves, without the inside. It comes properly when something's coming at me and I haven't got time to choose. When I ask for it on purpose, slowly, by the cot, it comes too, but badly. It's like copying out a word in somebody else's handwriting. You can see whose hand it was meant to be. Nobody would mistake it for theirs.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-33.md:85:He thought about the green book in Denvash. Three of them dead within weeks. He did not know what had happened to them. He knew only what had been written down, by somebody who had cared which kind of true got believed. But he thought, sitting under the pear tree, that whatever it had been, it had happened very fast, to people who had not yet had time to belong to anybody but the people they came from.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-31.md:83:It was not a seam. It was a wall, and it had been built on the second morning after Kindling, and he had not even known it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-31.md:125:He turned to the back of the volume. There were two appendices. The second began on a page that had come loose from the binding and been tucked back in, slightly crooked, by somebody's careful hand. At its head was a subheading he knew. He had copied it in a green book's handwriting at a table under a north window in Denvash, on the second morning after Kindling, and had not seen it since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:3:Nobody described Kindling the same way twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:11:*Like remembering something you never knew,* announced the girl from three streets over. She was two years past her own Kindling and explained it with the confidence of someone to whom a thing has happened exactly once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:33:This was the morning before his Kindling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:137:*[SHATTERED]: Classification indicating fractured Path architecture, historically associated with unstable practice and practitioner safety risk. Currently deprecated. Four (4) instances recorded in the history of the Compact registry.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:141:Not a percentage, and not one in a hundred thousand: four people, in the whole history of the Registry, and Cael, who wrote down everything, had not written this down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-01.md:227:Everyone in the northern quadrant knew Joren's result, so Cael did too. Joren had spent a year predicting his Kindling, starting with *something fast* and narrowing it week by week until, by midsummer, he was telling people at the market *Blade Path, Silver-track, probably Rank 3 by sixteen*. He had told Garrik, and Ressa, and even the guard on the Merchant Row gate, who had said *a door opening* and then *good luck, son* in a voice that made Cael wince. The whole quadrant had followed his certainty with the close attention people give to certainty they suspect is wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-48.md:269:He read them one at a time, with his finger under the line. *Assessor.* No. *Senior evaluation.* No. *Primary classification: the classification returned by the practitioner's Arbiter at Kindling and entered in the registry, including any entry made in the Supplementary Register.* He stopped. He read it again, and his finger did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-48.md:281:The category was not for anybody whose classification could not be read. It was for people who did not have one. Section four gave an assessor the power to put a practitioner into the category, and section one, three pages earlier, said who could be in it. A boy whose Arbiter had returned nothing would be in it. A boy whose return had been lost, or never recorded, would be in it. And a boy whose Arbiter had taken eleven seconds and then returned a word, a real word, a word with four instances and a statute of its own in the Supplementary Register, and a brass plate that had lit eleven seconds late, had a primary classification. He had more of one than anybody in the district. It simply was not one the standard framework could use.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-53.md:39:It had lain on the corner of his desk at regional for four weeks, under the window that looked onto the wall of the records building. It sat squared to the edge, as she had squared it when she put it there on the morning after she came back from Ardenmere, and he had read it on the first day twice, and again on the fourth, and then once a week or so since. Each time he had told himself that he was reading it for its procedure, which was very good. Each time he had read the procedure quickly and come to rest on the last paragraph but one, and stayed there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-53.md:255:The letter was on the end of the bench, where it had been for four weeks. It had come at the end of the second week of the spring, the one with the bone knife in it, and *you're right*, and Coss at the window, and a mark on a record that a careful woman could not trace. He had answered that letter in four lines within a day, because he had not been able to bear not answering it. He had not answered the question at the end of it. He had read the question every night since. He had answered the district letter, and said *I'm close*, and he had not been close. He had been sitting with the question as he sat with a figure that would not come out. It was a number he could not trace back to where it came from, and he would not sign it until he could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-21.md:33:Cael was not near enough to see its shape. He did not need to be. He had seen that shape pressed into dark wax on the second morning after Kindling, on a paper that was still folded small inside the back cover of his old notebook, with its creases gone soft from being opened. It was a hand, holding up a balance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-44.md:39:She talked for a quarter of an hour without stopping, and it was the most anybody had said to him at one time since Hesk on the night before Kindling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-02.md:5:He had copied it a month ago from the foot of the registry summary page, under the four-line entry he already knew by heart. It was a footnote, and it told anyone whose Kindling came out non-standard to present themselves at a Compact district office inside two days for something it called standard compliance processing. He had gone and found out what that processing involved, written it underneath, and struck both lines through. He never struck anything through. A wrong figure stayed on the page with the right one beside it, because Hesk said an erased mistake was a mistake you got to make again. This one he had crossed out anyway, and he still could not have said whether he had done it to be rid of it or so that he would always know where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-02.md:289:What followed took twenty minutes, and Cael would remember it longer than he remembered either bout. Garrik held the lamp hood open with a rag around his fingers. Hesk knelt on the floor with the side plate off the machine, and Cael held the hand lamp. Between the three of them they found the trouble. The little gauge Hesk had made, which told Garrik how tightly the film was being pulled, was reading true, but the spring behind the arm had tired, so that the arm sat a hair too low and let the film ride up off its sprocket. Hesk did not have a spring with him. Garrik did not have a spring anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-36.md:19:He looked for the anger, because he thought he might be owed some, and found instead something older and more tired that had his own face. He had kept a great many things in a great many neat columns for a great many years: Tuesdays, the provisions, the preliminary assessment, and a file he had once seen opened in a room above his own clearance. He had kept them from the boy until the boy found them out for himself and came to him angry, across the workshop bench, on the second day after Kindling. *A few years,* he had said in the side room at Weaver's Row, and he remembered the boy's face. If there was a figure missing from this letter, it was missing for the boy's reason. He would tell it when it was worth telling, or he would not, and either way it was not Hesk's to dig out of a letter with a file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-36.md:119:He had not spoken to her since the morning of the boy's Kindling, when they had passed her two blocks from home on the way back from Weaver's Row. She had been on the other side of the street with a basket, and she had lifted one open hand to the boy without crossing, and the boy had lifted his. He had heard from Cael's letters and from Ressa that she had gone to the healers', and had not thought about what that meant. It meant this: a fourteen-year-old girl carrying basins in an apron too big for her, with her sleeves pushed up past the elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-36.md:159:There were three of them at the table: the reviewing officer from the guild's own office, a thin careful man with a portfolio, whom Hesk had met once when he came to look at the workshop and ask about the drawer of springs. The other two were members chosen by lot, as the guild's rules required. One was a lens-grinder from the Inner District whom Hesk knew only by sight. The other was a woman he had sat near at thirty years of guild suppers without ever once learning her name, which he realized only as he sat down. She was a gauge-maker with a shop on the canal, a small fierce woman with a magnifier on a ribbon round her neck. She looked at his bound hand and raised her eyebrows and said nothing.

