editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-07.md:27:Every burst he had ever called had gone left. Every one he had measured on the straw, every one in twelve Copper bouts, every one this morning. But in the third exchange against Darrow Innes, with no ground left and his heel swept, the step had come up out of the dark unasked. It had gone the other way, to the right, round the edge of a blow where no step of his could have reached. It had gone where it was needed, not where it was used to going. He had written it down at the time, and drawn it, and never once looked at which side of the paper the figure was on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-06.md:63:*WHAT IT IS. Claim: a burst out of the way of a committed strike, faster than my legs. Evidence: the straw every morning; twelve Copper formals, Ulric the last. Ruling: so.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-01.md:115:"No. You did it to his training." She closed the book. "That's twelve Copper formals this year, by my count, and I'm the one who counts. Every one of them came in thinking six years of guild teaching was a wall you couldn't climb, and every one of them went home wondering who had moved it. You don't crow and you don't explain. You only keep doing it." She tilted her head. "It's a particular kind of insult, that. I'm not saying stop. I'm only telling you so you won't take it hard when one of them leaves your hand out in the air."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-07.md:79:He tried it slowly and quickly. He tried it standing and from a run. He tried it with Lira coming at him from the left, so that right was the only sensible way to go, in case the burst only needed a reason. He tried it from his right foot, in case it was the foot and not the hip. Every time, the drop of the right hip summoned nothing but a step, and every time the step was charged, a little, as if it had been the real thing. After the ninth try his breath was short in a way nine ordinary steps could never have made it, and his left hip, the one he had not been using, was warm and sore from the crest to the knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-06.md:73:*COSTS. Breath: half a breath held, every burst. The lock: two of Lira's* ands*, the same every time. Ten trials, nine bursts in one hour, five ways tried to shorten it, and none worked. Ruling: not a fault. A price, and it buys half a breath of only looking. Rule: the burst stays in my hip if anybody close enough to hit me has a hit I haven't priced. The hip: the left, crest to knee, and it adds up; the fruit woman can tell which day it was (h). Ceiling: three in an exchange, probable. Depletion: after nine in an hour, a cold hollow behind the breastbone, and the burst gone for two days. Ruling: once was enough to know.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:13:"I didn't ask if it was fair." She looked at his thigh, which he had wrapped, and then at his face, which he had not. "Ten, you said. Not eleven because you've had a good idea on the ninth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:153:He did not answer that. He got up, slowly, and his head went light, and the alcove tipped a little and came back. He went to the far wall for the ninth because he did not know what else to do. He came off it at a run, and dropped his left hip, and held his breath, and nothing happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:171:"Eight in an hour," he said from under his arm. "And the ninth wouldn't come at all. I've never spent eight in a day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:181:"Five of those were you trying to make it shorter," she said, "and it didn't get shorter, and you knew by the third one it wouldn't, and you did two more anyway because you'd come with five ideas and you didn't want to waste any. The seventh made me laugh, which I'll give you. The ninth didn't even come. That was your body telling you something." She looked at him steadily. "I'll do the tenth. I'll do it as hard as the first. But not so you can lie on the floor afterward with a tenth bruise and a tenth *same length*. Tell me what it's for, and I'll give it you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:185:He had been asking the half-breath one question all morning, and all month, and if he was honest, all the time since she had first told him it was there. *How do I get out of you?* He had asked it nine ways now, and eight times it had given him the same answer, counted aloud in Lira's breath, and the ninth time there had been no burst to ask it with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-05.md:275:He had found that out yesterday, on his back on a stone floor, at the cost of nine bursts and six hits and a lip. Hesk had known it all along, from a lifetime of housings, and had sent it over the hills in a letter that had been on the road for days. It had arrived the afternoon after Cael had paid for it himself. He did not mind. It was a strange kind of comfort to have paid full price for a thing and then find it written down, word for word, in a hand he loved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-08.md:59:It was a long letter for him. He told Hesk about the ten trials, and the five ways the lock would not get shorter, and the dance, and the ninth, when Lira would not hit a falling man. He told him about the tenth, and the exchange, and the rule. He told him what Hesk's own letter had said about the part that stays put, and how it had arrived the afternoon after he had found that out for himself on a stone floor, so that reading it had been like hearing the end of a song he had just sung.

